
  [1mWhat "persists across sessions" means determines whether the storage API can be synchronous.[0m A device-local store
  ([38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m) is synchronous — [38;5;153mprefs.get('rememberedUsername')[39m returns a value immediately, and the form can prefill
  during page load with no loading state. A server-backed store is inherently async (Promises everywhere, a loading
  state on the form, plus an auth'd endpoint that doesn't exist yet — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is currently a stub that never calls
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m).

  - [1mlocalStorage[0m — buys: works today, zero backend, sync API, trivial to test with a fake. Costs: per-browser, so
    preferences don't follow the user to another device; cleared by privacy tooling. Rules out nothing permanently, but
    swapping to server-backed later means converting the API to async and touching every caller.
  - [1mServer-backed[0m — buys: preferences follow the user across devices. Costs: requires a real auth'd backend we don't
    have, so most of this spec becomes API design against a stub. Async API from day one.
  - [1mlocalStorage now, async-shaped API[0m — sync storage under a Promise-returning interface so a later server swap doesn't
    churn callers. Buys future flexibility; costs a loading state and async complexity in the form for a migration that
    may never happen. This is the YAGNI-questionable middle.

  Recommendation: [1mlocalStorage with a sync API.[0m It's the only option that works against this codebase as it exists, and
  the adapter boundary in the design will keep the swap cost contained if a backend ever lands. I'd skip the pre-emptive
  async shaping.

  One thing I'll bake in regardless: the password never goes to storage — only the username.
